README.md:68:- **What's sent:** only the filtered, "significant" part of a diff (lockfiles, generated files, formatting-only changes, and anything under a size floor are excluded before generation ever runs) plus a list of programming concepts you've already answered questions about (so Grasp doesn't re-teach you the same thing). No full-repo access, no file reads beyond what's in the diff — Grasp explicitly runs the generation call with all tool access disabled.
README.md:93:- **`ignorePatterns`** — extra paths/filenames to never generate questions about, beyond the built-in list. **Matching is plain, not glob**: a pattern ending in `/` matches that name as a directory segment anywhere in the path (e.g. `"scripts/"` matches `scripts/build.sh` and `packages/a/scripts/x.js`); any other pattern matches an exact basename or an exact full relative path (e.g. `"generated.ts"` matches both `generated.ts` and `src/generated.ts`, but not `generated.test.ts`). Wildcards like `*` or `**` are **not** supported and are matched literally, so `"generated/**"` will silently match nothing — use `"generated/"` instead.
README.md:103:- **`hard`:** Grasp will deny the next tool call in a Claude Code session if that session has an unanswered question outstanding, until you answer or explicitly skip it via `grasp review`. This only reaches into the session that produced the question — it never blocks you over something left over from a different day or a different project. Turn this on deliberately; it's a real interruption, and the whole design philosophy here is "teach, don't punish" — soft is the safer default for exactly that reason.
DECISIONS.md:33:**Reasoning:** JSON needs no extra parsing dependency in a Node/TS project (`JSON.parse` is enough), and it's the format Claude Code's own settings already use, so it stays consistent with the ecosystem Grasp lives in. A two-tier global + per-repo merge matches how the brief actually talks about config (ignore patterns, caps) — some of it is clearly personal/global (cost cap, gate mode) and some is naturally project-specific (ignore patterns for a given repo's generated paths), so a single flat file would force one scope to win awkwardly over the other.
DECISIONS.md:81:**Decision:** No separate regex/path-based detector for §5.2's "prefer hunks touching flagged patterns" language. This judgment is folded entirely into the Phase 5 judge+generate call. Phase 4 stays purely mechanical: size thresholds, lockfiles/generated paths, ignore patterns — no semantic pattern-matching lives there.
DECISIONS.md:125:**Alternatives considered:** Pin to an older Node LTS instead of upgrading the dependency — rejected because it would mean asking every future contributor/user to manage a specific Node version rather than fixing a library version pin, and the project doesn't otherwise need anything Node-26-specific. Patch or vendor `better-sqlite3@11` locally — rejected as needless maintenance burden when a newer major already solves it cleanly upstream. `package.json`'s `engines.node` is left at `>=18` since `13.x`'s own stated floor is Node `>=22`; noting here in case that engines field needs tightening later if a real user hits it on an older Node.
DECISIONS.md:133:**Reasoning:** CommonJS avoids the ESM/CJS interop friction that `better-sqlite3` (a native CJS addon) and a `#!/usr/bin/env node` shebang bin script would otherwise create — Node's ESM loader has extra rules around `.js` extensions in relative imports and native addon resolution that CommonJS sidesteps entirely, and Claude Code's own hook scripts are plain CommonJS-friendly Node too. ES2022 as the emit target is the newest spec level fully supported by Node 18 (the floor `engines.node` currently declares), so it uses modern syntax (top-level `??=`, `Object.hasOwn`, etc. if ever needed) without pushing runtime support past what the package claims to support.
DECISIONS.md:149:## 2026-08-04 — Per-repo config resolution: cwd only, no upward search
DECISIONS.md:153:**Reasoning:** Phase 1 has no git/repo-detection logic yet — that machinery doesn't exist until the capture adapters in Phases 2–3 — so implementing upward search now would mean guessing at how "repo root" gets defined before the phase that actually owns that definition has been built. Resolving against the exact directory Grasp is invoked from is also simplest to reason about and test today, and does not foreclose adding upward search later: Phase 2/3 can pass whatever directory they determine to be the repo root into this same `loadConfig(repoRoot)` signature without changing this function at all.
DECISIONS.md:155:**Alternatives considered:** Walk up from `cwd` to the nearest `.git` directory, `.grasp.json`, or filesystem root (the common pattern for tools like ESLint/Prettier) — more convenient for a user running `grasp` from a subdirectory, but requires deciding *now* what "repo root" means, which duplicates work the capture-adapter phases will need to do anyway with actual git integration. Deferred rather than rejected outright — likely to be revisited once Phase 2/3 need a real repo-root concept.
DECISIONS.md:173:**Reasoning:** Three flat, mutually-exclusive commands with no options/flags of their own don't justify a parsing library; a manual switch is fewer lines and one fewer dependency to audit for an open-source tool where §3.6 already treats trust as load-bearing. This isn't meant to hold for later phases — once real commands need flags, subcommands, and `--help` text generated per-command (Phase 7+, once the TUI and gate config surface grow), a proper CLI framework is likely worth revisiting.
DECISIONS.md:195:**Alternatives considered:** Diff against the index only (`git diff`, no ref) — simplest, and what the brief's own example command shows, but misses any changes an agent (or the user) happens to have staged, which a pure "diff HEAD" comparison doesn't; rejected as strictly less complete for the same complexity. A stateful "diff between checkpoints" — store a snapshot (tree hash, or full file contents) at the end of each capture and diff against it next time, matching the brief's literal "between checkpoints" phrasing more closely — was considered and set aside for this phase specifically because there's no poll loop or persistent capture-daemon yet for "checkpoints" to mean anything; `HEAD` is the closest stateless proxy available today, and a real checkpoint concept is more naturally Phase 3's problem once hooks define actual event boundaries (`PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse`/`Stop`) to checkpoint against.
DECISIONS.md:263:**Decision:** `resolvePromptId()` (`src/adapters/claudeCodeAdapter.ts`) falls back to a single fixed sentinel key (`"__no_prompt_id__"`) whenever a hook payload's `prompt_id` is missing or empty — never a freshly-generated random id per invocation.
DECISIONS.md:283:**Decision:** A hunk is formatting-only if its normalized sequence of removed lines exactly equals its normalized sequence of added lines (same content, same order). "Normalize" means: strip the leading `+`/`-` marker, trim the line, and collapse any run of internal whitespace to a single space. A file is formatting-only only if *every* one of its hunks is. Comparison is by ordered sequence, not by multiset/sorted comparison.
DECISIONS.md:293:**Decision:** `diffThresholds.minChangedLines` is evaluated per file (after ignore-pattern and formatting-only exclusion): the diff as a whole clears the minimum if *at least one* remaining file has `insertions + deletions >= minChangedLines`. It is not evaluated as a sum across all files. The maximum thresholds (`maxTotalChangedLines`, `maxSingleFileChangedLines`), by contrast, unambiguously apply to the sum across files and to any individual file respectively — that reading was already settled by the Phase 1 "Diff-size thresholds" entry's own wording ("skip diffs over 1500 total ... or 800 ... in any single file").
DECISIONS.md:305:**Reasoning:** The Phase 1 baseline list itself already mixes both shapes (`"package-lock.json"` — a bare filename meant to match wherever it occurs, since lockfiles commonly exist in subdirectories in monorepos — and `"node_modules/"`/`"dist/"` — directory names meant to match at any depth), so the matcher has to support both regardless of what's chosen for user patterns; extending the same two rules to `ignorePatterns` keeps one matching function instead of two. Going further to full glob support would need a dependency (`minimatch`/`picomatch`) or a hand-rolled glob engine, for a phase whose brief (§5.2) only asks for "lockfiles, generated files ... gitignored paths" — patterns that are all expressible as plain names or directory segments — not arbitrary wildcards. This is the same "keep the mechanical layer dumb, no more machinery than the task in front of it needs" reasoning already applied to git access and diff parsing in Phase 2.
DECISIONS.md:431:**Reasoning:** Verified empirically before committing to anything, per this project's standing discipline: a plain `await import("ink")` written directly in a `.ts` file compiled under this project's `tsconfig.json` (`"module": "CommonJS"`) gets *downleveled by TypeScript itself* into `Promise.resolve().then(() => require("ink"))` — confirmed by inspecting the emitted JS — which fails at runtime with Node's CJS loader raising `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`/`ERR_REQUIRE_ESM`-class errors, because `require()` cannot load a pure-ESM package synchronously no matter how it's wrapped. Wrapping the same call in `eval('import("ink")')` sidesteps TypeScript's static rewriting entirely (TS doesn't parse the contents of a string literal passed to `eval`), so Node's own runtime module loader receives a genuine dynamic `import()` — confirmed by testing both paths back to back and observing the second one hit Node's *ESM* resolver (`ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND`) instead of the CJS one. This is a well-known, narrow workaround for exactly this situation (a CJS TypeScript project needing one ESM-only dependency) and keeps the blast radius to a single loader file, rather than re-opening the Phase 1 module-system decision — which was made for reasons (native `better-sqlite3` CJS addon, shebang-script simplicity) that are still true and unrelated to `ink`. `ink@5`/`react@18` (rather than the current `ink@7`/`react@19`) was picked for peer-dependency stability: `ink@5` only requires `react >=18`, a much more broadly-compatible and battle-tested React major than the `>=19.2.0` `ink@7` demands, and `ink@5` is still a fully current, actively-used major (not the legacy `ink@3` line, which is the *only* other option with native CJS support but is four-plus years stale).
DECISIONS.md:433:**Alternatives considered:** Convert the whole project to ESM (`"type": "module"`, `"module": "NodeNext"`) — rejected as reopening a settled, still-valid Phase 1 decision for the sake of one new dependency; would also touch every existing file's import style and the `__dirname`-based `readPackageVersion()` logic in `cli.ts` for no benefit unrelated to this phase's actual goal. Pin to `ink@3` (last version with a plain CJS build, `require("ink")` works with no interop shim needed) — rejected as needlessly stale (over four years without updates, missing the Yoga layout engine and general stability work of ink 4/5/6/7) purely to avoid a five-line interop helper. Use Node's newer "require(esm)" support directly (`require("ink")` working natively in-process on very recent Node) — rejected as version-fragile: that support landed only in recent Node versions and isn't reliable across the `engines.node: >=18` floor this package already commits to, whereas `eval`-wrapped dynamic `import()` works on any Node version with ESM support at all (well below the 18 floor).
DECISIONS.md:461:**Reasoning:** `session_id`-scoping (not global) matches the cost cap's own already-established boundary (Phase 5) and, independently, is the only scope that doesn't risk the exact "punishment tool" failure mode brief §3.1 warns about: if hard-gate checked *globally*, an unanswered question left over from a repo touched days ago could block work in a completely unrelated project today — a user would experience that as arbitrary and unfair, not as "teach, don't punish." Scoping to *this* session's own outstanding work is what "next command blocked until answered" (brief §3.1) actually describes — you shouldn't be able to keep telling Claude Code to do more work in the same sitting that produced an unanswered question, but an old question from a different sitting shouldn't reach into today's work at all. Not narrowing further to just the current turn (`prompt_id`) matters too: `Stop` fires once per turn, so if turn 1 generates a question and the user hasn't answered it by turn 2's `PreToolUse`, a turn-scoped check would let them simply move on and never be asked again within that sitting — checking the whole `session_id` (every turn so far) is what makes hard-gate actually mean "must engage before proceeding," not "can dodge by starting a new turn."
DECISIONS.md:463:**Alternatives considered:** Global scope (any pending question anywhere blocks any tool call anywhere) — rejected per the cross-project-punishment reasoning above. Per-turn (`(session_id, prompt_id)`) scope — rejected because it would let a user escape an unanswered question from an earlier turn simply by continuing to a new one in the same session, undermining hard-gate's actual purpose. Repo-scoped (any pending question for this repo, regardless of session) — considered, since it's a plausible middle ground, but rejected as solving a problem `session_id`-scoping already solves more precisely: two different sessions in the same repo on different days are exactly the kind of "different sitting" hard-gate shouldn't reach across, the same reasoning that ruled out global scope.
DECISIONS.md:509:**Decision:** `grasp review`'s pending-question list (`groupForBatchPresentation`, `src/review.ts`) is grouped by `session_id` before rendering: all of one session's pending questions stay adjacent, session-groups themselves are ordered by when each group's earliest question was generated, and questions within a group stay oldest-first (inherited from `getPendingQuestions`' existing timestamp-ASC order — no separate re-sort needed there). Events with a null `session_id` (currently only possible via `debug:seed`) each become their own singleton group rather than being merged with each other or interleaved into a real session's group. The rendered header shows the overall position ("N of M pending") plus, whenever there's more than one question in the current session's group or more than one session in the batch, a session-context line ("session X of Y — question A of B for this session").
DECISIONS.md:511:**Reasoning:** The task's own framing is "make a real accumulated batch read coherently, not like an arbitrary list" — a flat global timestamp order (what Phase 7 shipped, and what `getPendingQuestions` alone still returns) actively works against that whenever more than one session contributes pending questions: two sessions' questions would end up interleaved by whenever each happened to be generated, forcing the user to mentally context-switch between unrelated diffs every other question instead of working through one coherent unit of related work before moving to the next. Grouping by `session_id` is the natural unit because it's also the boundary every other cap/scope decision in this project already uses (cost cap, question cap, hard-gate check) — reusing it here rather than inventing a different grouping key (e.g. by repo, or by day) keeps "session" as the one consistent notion of "a coherent unit of related work" throughout the whole codebase. Ordering *groups* by earliest-question-in-group (rather than, say, alphabetically by session id, or by group size) preserves the same "oldest first" intuition the flat list already had, just applied one level up — a user working through a real backlog naturally expects to clear older work first. This is presentation logic, not a query concern, so it lives in `review.ts` (which already owns TUI wiring) operating on `getPendingQuestions`' existing global, timestamp-ordered result, rather than pushing a `GROUP BY`/window-function query into `store.ts` — the store's job is still just "give me every pending question," and how that gets organized for one specific presentation surface is `review`'s own concern, matching Phase 7's existing store/UI split.
DECISIONS.md:531:**Reasoning:** `grasp init` is the one command in the entire codebase that both (a) has genuine interactive terminal access (it's a real, user-launched CLI invocation, same category as `grasp review` — not a hook subprocess) and (b) is a hard prerequisite for generation ever running at all: `runGeneration` only ever executes from inside `ClaudeCodeAdapter.onChangeDetected`, which only ever runs from a `PostToolUse` hook firing, which can only fire if Claude Code has the hook registered — and the only code path that registers it is `grasp init` writing `.claude/settings.local.json`. There is no sequence of events in which a real generation call happens without `grasp init` having run and been confirmed first, so "before any question could possibly be generated" (this phase's own verification-step wording) is not just plausible but structurally guaranteed by where the message already sits, not merely likely. Every other candidate location fails one of the two properties: `ensureInitialized()` (runs on literally every `grasp` invocation, including `--version`/`--help`) has no interactive gate and would print the same disclosure on commands that have nothing to do with generation, training users to skim past it before they ever reach the one moment it actually matters; a hook itself (`PreToolUse`/`Stop`) has no controlling terminal (Phase 7's finding) and fires *after* the moment consent should have happened, not before.
DECISIONS.md:543:**Alternatives considered:** Exclude `.grasp.json` at the git-diff-capture layer instead of the filter layer (i.e., never let `captureGitDiff` pick it up as an untracked file in the first place) — rejected because Phase 2's untracked-file union is deliberately adapter-agnostic and content-blind (it doesn't know what "Grasp's own config" means, only "files git sees"); teaching the filter layer, which already owns exactly this kind of "files that don't count" judgment for lockfiles/generated paths, keeps the special-casing in one place instead of two. Have `grasp init`/first config-write add `.grasp.json` to the *user's own* `ignorePatterns` array automatically — rejected as solving a universal problem (every install has this file) with a per-repo, user-editable mechanism a user could accidentally remove, when a baseline entry is unconditional and requires no such bookkeeping. Leave it as user-facing guidance ("remember to gitignore your `.grasp.json`") instead of a code fix — considered, since `captureGitDiff`'s untracked-file listing does use `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` (confirmed by testing: adding `.grasp.json` to a scratch repo's `.gitignore` does stop it appearing in the untracked-file list), so gitignoring it per-repo would in fact have worked. Rejected anyway because it pushes a universal problem (every install has this file, every time) onto per-user diligence a new user has no way to know they need — the same reasoning that already put lockfiles/`node_modules/` in the baseline list rather than expecting every user's own `.gitignore` to cover them; a baseline fix protects every install unconditionally, a documentation-only fix protects only the installs that happened to read and follow it.
DECISIONS.md:551:**Reasoning:** This directly fixes Codex's #1 critical finding: every `PostToolUse` re-diffing the whole tree against `HEAD` meant two firings with no new work between them produced the same captured diff, the same generated question, and the same charge twice, and could attribute pre-existing uncommitted work (present before the agent's session even started) to the agent. The brief itself (§5.1) describes the fallback capture path as "watch git working-tree state / diff between checkpoints," and Phase 2's own "Git-diff capture: comparison mechanism" entry explicitly flagged this as deferred rather than solved ("there's no poll loop or persistent capture-daemon yet for 'checkpoints' to mean anything... a real checkpoint concept is more naturally Phase 3's problem once hooks define actual event boundaries to checkpoint against") — Phase 3 then built the hook plumbing but never actually closed that loop, which is exactly the gap this fixes now that a real trigger (`PostToolUse`) and a real state-persistence layer (SQLite, already used for `cc_turns`/`captured_diffs`) both exist. The git-tree-object mechanism specifically (rather than a raw file-content snapshot or a plain hash comparison) was chosen after direct empirical testing, not assumption: a naive alternative — `git diff <tree>` against the live worktree — was tried first and found to **misreport untracked files as deleted**, because that form of `git diff` is actually a tree-vs-*index* comparison under the hood (augmented by worktree stat info for paths already in the index), and the real index never has untracked entries regardless of what the checkpoint tree contains. Diffing two independently-built tree snapshots against each other sidesteps the index entirely and was confirmed correct for modifications, additions, deletions, and untracked-file transitions before being wired into the adapter. Building the tree via a scratch `GIT_INDEX_FILE` (not the real index) matters because it's the standard, safe technique for snapshotting a worktree without ever making Grasp's own bookkeeping visible to `git status`/`git commit` — the repo's real index is never touched. Seeding the checkpoint on the *first hook firing of any type* (not lazily inside `checkAndCapture`) matters because `checkAndCapture` only runs on `PostToolUse`, i.e. *after* a tool call already ran; seeding there would bake that first tool call's own work into the "nothing new yet" baseline and silently lose it. In ordinary Claude Code operation `PreToolUse` fires before the first tool call, so seeding unconditionally at the top of every hook (same place `ensureTurnStarted` already runs, itself idempotent) reliably captures the true pre-agent-work state.
DECISIONS.md:601:**Decision:** Added a real automated test suite (51 tests) using Node's built-in test runner (`node:test` + `node:assert/strict`) — no new devDependency. Tests live in `test/*.test.ts` (plus `test/fixtures/`), compiled by a dedicated `tsconfig.test.json` (extends the main config, `rootDir: "."`, `outDir: "test-dist"`) so compiled test output never mixes into the real `dist/` that gets published. `npm test` runs `tsc -p tsconfig.test.json && node --test test-dist/test/*.test.js`. Coverage: the mechanical filter (`test/filter.test.ts` — size thresholds, formatting-only detection, ignore-pattern matching including baseline entries), judge-response parsing and timeout detection (`test/generation.test.ts` — `parseJudgeResponse`, `isTimeoutError`, `buildDiffSummary`), the cost/question cap math end-to-end including the missing-cost fix above (`test/caps.test.ts` — real `openStore` + a controllable mock `claude` fixture on `PATH`, the same technique this project's manual verification has used throughout, now automated), config merge/precedence (`test/config.test.ts` — `deepMerge`, `ensureGlobalConfigFile`, `loadConfig` against temp directories), and the SQLite concurrency fix (`test/concurrency.test.ts`, covered in its own entry above). Three small, narrowly-scoped exports were added purely for testability, all backward-compatible (every existing call site is unaffected): `deepMerge`, `parseJudgeResponse`, and `isTimeoutError` became exported instead of module-private, and `ensureGlobalConfigFile`/`loadConfig` gained optional `globalConfigPath`/`graspHome` parameters (defaulting to the real `~/.grasp` locations) so tests can point them at a temp directory instead of ever touching a real user's config.
DECISIONS.md:611:**Decision:** `grasp init`'s idempotency check now classifies each of `PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse`/`Stop` into one of three states — no Grasp entry yet ("missing"), a Grasp entry present but structurally different from what a fresh install would write today ("stale"), or already correct — instead of only ever checking "does a Grasp entry exist at all." Missing entries are added and stale entries are replaced in place (`findGraspEntryIndex` locates the specific array entry to swap, leaving any other, non-Grasp entries for the same event untouched). The confirmation message shows a `before`/`after` for every stale entry, following the same show-then-confirm pattern the original missing-entry install already used, and the prompt becomes "Apply these changes?" (covering both add and update) rather than "Add these hooks?".

